== Games and Movies

There are basically two "modes" in which we generate 3D graphics:
- *Pre-rendered*: we know what images we will need in advance, such as the frames of a movie. We have time to *render* (draw) these images, so that when we're ready, they're easy to display on the screen. Obviously, faster is better, but it can take hours and still work.
- *Realtime*: we _don't_ know the images we will need in advance. They depend on something, such as user input or randomness. This is the case for video games.

What do I mean by _not knowing_ the images in advance?

