== A video game "frame" (2)

A single image that is part of a larger animated series is called a *frame*.

That frame was produced in response to extremely precise circumstances. 

If the camera had been slightly different, if the player had approached the enemy slightly differently, if they had a different number of lives or amount of health, the image would be different.

If it takes too long (more than 1/30th of a second) to make the image, the game becomes sluggish and hard to control. 

== Performance is critical

Therefore performance is absolutely critical.

That game was for the Nintendo 64, by modern standards a very primitive system. And yet, it was able to produce those images (somewhat) quickly using specialized hardware.

Using this hardware requires special programming techniques.

So now, let's talk history. How have these techniques and hardware capabilities emerged?

